{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is Go concurrency?

**Go concurrency** is a way to structure a program as independently progressing
tasks. A **goroutine** runs one task, a **channel** transfers ownership of a
value or signal, and the Go runtime schedules runnable goroutines across
operating-system threads.

Concurrency is not the same as parallelism:

- **Concurrency** means several tasks can make progress over the same period.
- **Parallelism** means several tasks execute at the same instant on different
  CPU cores.
- An I/O service can benefit from high concurrency while many goroutines wait.
- A CPU-bound service usually gains useful parallelism only up to its available
  execution capacity.

The production invariant is simple: **every goroutine needs an owner, a bounded
resource, and a finite exit path**. Cheap goroutines make structure convenient;
they do not make queues, memory, CPU, or dependencies unlimited.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card %}
## Build the runtime mental model

A goroutine starts with a small stack that can grow and shrink. When it blocks
on a channel, timer, network poller, or synchronization primitive, the runtime
can schedule another runnable goroutine. This multiplexing is why a Go process
can manage far more concurrent tasks than operating-system threads.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Goroutine" eyebrow="Unit of work" icon="code" tone="blue" %}
Created with `go f()`. Its lifetime is independent from the caller unless the
program deliberately connects them with a context, result, or join.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Channel" eyebrow="Handoff and signal" icon="route" tone="violet" %}
A typed synchronization point. A send and receive transfer a value while the
buffer determines how far producer and consumer timing can diverge.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Scheduler" eyebrow="Maps work to threads" icon="layers" tone="green" %}
The runtime maps runnable goroutines onto a bounded set of execution resources.
Blocked work can yield; CPU-bound work still consumes real cores.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

Go's channel-oriented style was influenced by **Communicating Sequential
Processes (CSP)**: independent processes coordinate through communication. Go
does not require every state update to use a channel; mutexes and atomics remain
appropriate when shared ownership expresses the invariant more clearly.

{% system-flow title="One concurrent request path" caption="The request owns cancellation; admission and workers bound how much work enters the dependency." %}
{% system-node title="Request" eyebrow="Owner" icon="users" tone="neutral" %}
Carries a deadline and cancellation signal for work that becomes useless when
the request ends.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Admission" eyebrow="Bound" icon="shield" tone="blue" %}
Accepts, waits for capacity, or rejects. It prevents demand from silently
becoming an unbounded number of goroutines.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Worker pool" eyebrow="Execution" icon="server" tone="violet" %}
Runs a measured number of jobs and stops receiving when the owner cancels.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Dependency" eyebrow="Downstream budget" icon="database" tone="green" %}
Receives the remaining deadline instead of starting a new independent lifetime.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

`GOMAXPROCS` controls how many operating-system threads may execute Go code at
the same time. It does not cap goroutine count, queue depth, open connections,
or work admitted to a dependency.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="cyan" %}
## Size useful concurrency before choosing a pool

**Useful concurrency** is the average work that must be in flight to sustain the
target arrival rate. Little's Law provides the first estimate:

`in-flight work = arrival rate × time in the system`

At 1,000 requests per second and 80 ms average service time, the mean is about
80 requests in flight. Production capacity needs additional headroom for
latency tails, bursts, retries, garbage collection, and partial failures.

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric value="λ" label="Arrival rate" detail="New jobs or requests per second" tone="blue" /%}
{% metric value="W" label="Time in system" detail="Queue wait plus active service time" tone="violet" /%}
{% metric value="L = λW" label="In-flight demand" detail="Average useful concurrency before safety headroom" tone="cyan" /%}
{% metric value="Bound" label="Admission limit" detail="The explicit overload and dependency protection contract" tone="amber" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

The estimate exposes different limits:

- For **I/O-bound work**, enough concurrency hides waiting time, but database
  connections, sockets, queue age, and remote quotas usually become the bound.
- For **CPU-bound work**, throughput approaches the available CPU budget.
  Additional runnable goroutines add waiting and scheduler pressure.
- For **mixed work**, measure CPU time and wall time under representative load;
  neither thread count nor average latency alone reveals the bottleneck.
- For every profile, use a bounded queue or semaphore so overload has a visible
  result instead of consuming memory until the process fails.

### Lab 1: find the controlling capacity

Select a workload shape, then change arrival rate, service time, and worker
limit. Reach both a healthy state and an overloaded state. Notice when adding
workers changes throughput and when CPU remains the controlling resource.

{% interactive-block id="technology/go-concurrency-capacity-lab" dataFile="/api/content/technology/go-concurrency/data/concurrency-capacity-model.json" /%}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card %}
## Use channels as ownership boundaries

A channel is not a general-purpose queue. It is an in-process handoff with
synchronization semantics.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Unbuffered" eyebrow="Rendezvous" icon="route" tone="blue" %}
A send completes only when a receiver is ready. This tightly couples producer
and consumer progress and is useful when the handoff itself is the contract.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Buffered" eyebrow="Bounded timing gap" icon="layers" tone="amber" %}
A sender can get ahead by at most the buffer capacity. The buffer absorbs a
short mismatch; it does not create processing capacity or durable storage.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Directional" eyebrow="API constraint" icon="shield" tone="green" %}
Expose `<-chan T` to receivers and `chan<- T` to senders. Direction documents
ownership and lets the compiler reject accidental use.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

Follow three channel rules:

1. The sender that knows no more values will be produced owns `close`.
2. Receivers use the second value from a receive, or `range`, to detect closure.
3. Every potentially blocking send or receive on a request path needs a
   cancellation or shutdown case.

Closing broadcasts that no more values will arrive; it does not cancel work,
flush a queue, or make concurrent senders safe. Sending on a closed channel
panics, while receiving from a closed channel returns remaining buffered values
and then the zero value.

{% callout variant="warn" %}
Do not close a channel from a receiver merely because that receiver is done.
Other senders may still own valid work. Express receiver cancellation through a
context or a separate signal and let the sending owner close the data channel.
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Propagate cancellation through the whole work graph

A **context** carries cancellation, deadlines, and request-scoped values across
an API boundary. It should be the first parameter, should not be stored in a
long-lived struct, and should reach downstream operations that can stop early.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Create one lifetime" label="Owner" %}
Derive a context at the request, batch, or service boundary. Call its cancel
function so timers and child resources are released.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Make admission cancellable" label="Before work" %}
Use `select` when acquiring a semaphore or sending to a jobs channel. A cancelled
caller must not remain blocked while trying to start work.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Pass the remaining budget" label="During work" %}
Workers and database, HTTP, or RPC calls receive the same context. Do not replace
it with `context.Background()` inside the operation.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Join and close outputs" label="After work" %}
Wait for producers or workers, close outputs exactly once, and make every result
consumer terminate on closure or cancellation.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

The cancellation path matters most when the happy path is slow. A goroutine can
leak while blocked on:

- a send whose receiver returned early;
- a receive whose producer exited without closing;
- a lock or semaphore with no deadline;
- a timer or ticker that is never stopped;
- a dependency call created from a detached context;
- a result channel nobody will read after a request timeout.

### Lab 2: trace cancellation and backpressure

Inject a client timeout, downstream stall, or producer burst. Compare detached
goroutines with worker-only cancellation and end-to-end structured cancellation.
Change the buffer and worker count, then inspect where blocked or abandoned work
survives.

